---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Frequently asked questions about scrobbling — Scrobble.dev';
const description = 'Why anime has a separate catalogue facet, how common trackers differ, what portable scrobble records preserve, and how catalogue data can be reused.';
const questions = [
  ['Why is anime a separate catalogue facet?', 'Anime is a separate filter because it helps people find projects that track episodes, specials, split releases, continuing titles and rewatches. It is a navigation choice, not a claim that anime sits outside film or television.'],
  ['How do common tracking projects differ?', 'Projects differ in media scope, capture method, hosting model and the records they keep. Some receive playback events, some provide a diary, some keep listening history, and some store a self-hosted record across several media.'],
  ['What should a portable scrobble preserve?', 'A portable record should preserve stable event identity, source-local media identity, event and recorded times, native progress, provenance, correction or deletion state, and enough retry information to avoid duplicate history.'],
  ['Can the Scrobble.dev catalogue be reused commercially?', 'Yes. Scrobble.dev releases the catalogue under CC0. That dedication covers the catalogue published by Scrobble.dev; linked projects, repositories and upstream data retain their own terms.'],
  ['How can I contribute?', 'Use the Scrobble.dev issue tracker for a factual correction or project update. Use the Floppy or FloppyDesktop issue tracker for changes to those applications. Include the claim, primary source and date checked.']
];
const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': 'https://scrobble.dev/faq/#questions',
    mainEntity: questions.map(([name, text]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text } }))
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Scrobble.dev', item: 'https://scrobble.dev/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Frequently asked questions', item: 'https://scrobble.dev/faq/' }
    ]
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} type="WebPage" schema={schema} markdown="/knowledge/faq.md">
  <article class="shell section article h-entry">
    <div class="article-body e-content">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Field guide</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">FAQ</span></nav>
      <p class="kicker">Frequently asked questions</p>
      <h1 class="p-name">Questions about scrobbling, trackers and portable records.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst e-summary">Start with the practical difference. Follow the project link when an answer depends on current behaviour.</p>

      <h2 id="anime">Why is anime a separate catalogue facet?</h2>
      <p>Anime is a separate filter because it helps people find projects that track episodes, specials, split releases, continuing titles and rewatches. It is a navigation choice, not a claim that anime sits outside film or television.</p>
      <p>A project can appear under anime and another media type. The facet should remain only while it helps people find relevant tools and tracking behaviour.</p>

      <h2 id="trackers">How do common tracking projects differ?</h2>
      <p>Projects differ in media scope, capture method, hosting model and the records they keep. Some receive playback events, some provide a diary, some keep listening history, and some store a self-hosted record across several media.</p>
      <p class="catalogue-scroll-hint" id="tracker-table-hint">Swipe the table sideways to compare every column.</p>
      <div class="catalogue-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Common tracker comparison" aria-describedby="tracker-table-hint" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Project</th><th scope="col">Main scope</th><th scope="col">Typical record</th><th scope="col">Useful when</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/dannyvfilms/Floppy">Floppy</a></th><td>Ten media types</td><td>Library, history and progress</td><td>A self-hosted, cross-media record is required.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.last.fm/about/trackmymusic">Last.fm</a></th><td>Music</td><td>Submitted listens</td><td>Players and services submit listening activity.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://listenbrainz.org/">ListenBrainz</a></th><td>Music</td><td>Open listening history</td><td>An open-source service and exportable listening data are required.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://trakt.tv/">Trakt</a></th><td>Film and television</td><td>History, progress and playback events</td><td>Media applications report playback activity.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://simkl.com/">Simkl</a></th><td>Film, television and anime</td><td>History, lists and episode state</td><td>One service covers its three advertised media areas.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://letterboxd.com/welcome/">Letterboxd</a></th><td>Film</td><td>Dated diary entry, rating, review or list</td><td>The task is film logging rather than live playback progress.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/wetrakr/wetrakr-kodi">WeTrakr</a></th><td>Film and television</td><td>Kodi playback events</td><td>Kodi playback is sent to a tracking service.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The <a href="/projects/">project catalogue</a> records source availability, licences and contribution routes. It describes projects; it does not rank them.</p>

      <h2 id="portable-record">What should a portable scrobble preserve?</h2>
      <p>A portable record should preserve enough information to identify, interpret, retry and correct the event:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A stable event identifier.</li>
        <li>The media type and the source-local identity known when the event was recorded.</li>
        <li>The event time and, when different, the recorded time.</li>
        <li>Progress in the medium's native unit before a percentage is derived.</li>
        <li>Provenance, including whether the record came from playback, manual entry, import or synchronization.</li>
        <li>Correction or deletion state and enough retry information to prevent duplicate history.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These fields do not guarantee that two systems will make the same decision. They preserve enough context to explain a difference and correct the resulting history.</p>

      <h2 id="licensing">Can the Scrobble.dev catalogue be reused commercially?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Scrobble.dev releases its project catalogue under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">CC0</a>, which permits copying, modification and commercial reuse without asking Scrobble.dev for permission.</p>
      <p>That dedication covers the catalogue published by Scrobble.dev. Linked projects, repositories and upstream data retain their own terms. A software licence does not automatically grant rights to data used by that software. The <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/protection-databases">European Commission's database-protection summary</a> explains the separate rights that may apply in the European Union. This is an editorial summary, not legal advice.</p>

      <h2 id="contribute">How can I contribute?</h2>
      <p>For a factual correction or project update, <a href="https://github.com/Electric-Town/scrobble.dev/issues/new">open a Scrobble.dev issue</a>. Include the claim, a primary source and the date checked. The <a href="/about/">editorial method</a> explains how claims and draft proposals are labelled.</p>
      <p>For application changes, use the <a href="https://github.com/dannyvfilms/Floppy/issues">Floppy issue tracker</a> or the <a href="https://github.com/Electric-Town/FloppyDesktop/issues">FloppyDesktop issue tracker</a>. Include the media type, event sequence, observed result, expected result and a private-data-safe example.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <strong>On this page</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#anime">Why anime?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#trackers">Tracker differences</a></li>
        <li><a href="#portable-record">Portable records</a></li>
        <li><a href="#licensing">Catalogue licensing</a></li>
        <li><a href="#contribute">Contribute</a></li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
